+**THE RATIO PASS (locked aa) — this one is NOT a rescale, and that is the point.** The Doubling
+kept every ratio; this changes them deliberately, because the thinness was never a magnitude
+problem. At 8 HP an Archer died to two collisions, so there was no such thing as "a little
+damage" — **gradients were arithmetically impossible**, and a wound could not be carried across
+four nodes.
+
+*Considered and rejected: a flat pool (50) across all four ducks.* The 24-vs-42 spread is not
+incidental — it is what makes the Archer's minimum range a real tax, the Fisher's reach a real
+trade, and the Wardbearer's stance worth having. Flattening it would move class differentiation
+out of positioning and into nothing, and §2's "durability lives inside decisions" cuts the other
+way here: the stat line is what makes the *decision* different per class. If uniform pools are ever
+wanted, differentiation has to move somewhere already load-bearing (resist values, the halving) —
+a bigger redesign, and its own session.
+
+| Change | From → To | Why |
+|---|---|---|
+| **Player HP ×3** | Archer/Fisher 8→**24**, Vanguard/Wardbearer 14→**42** | Player HP is a RUN resource. A collision goes from **half** an Archer to a **quarter**, so chip damage means something and a wound can be carried across four nodes rather than two. ×2 was tried on paper and rejected as too conservative: at 16 an Archer still died to three collisions, which is better arithmetic but still not an attrition medium. **The fragility gradient is preserved on purpose** — the ratio between fragile and tank is unchanged, because 24-vs-42 is what makes the Archer's minimum range a real tax and the Wardbearer a rock in the stream. A flat pool across all four would delete that axis |
+| **Enemy HP ×1.5** | Husk 4→**6**, Lobber/Perch 6→**8**, Stalker/Harrier 8→**12**, Grappler/Bulwark 10→**14**, Anchor/Warden 12→**18**, Colossus 20→**30**, Raider 4→**6**. **Runt stays 2** | Enemy HP is a FIGHT resource. Enemies now persist across rounds, so board state evolves instead of resetting after every action |
+| **Unit collision 4 → 6** | | **Not a new number** — brambles, spikes and structure collisions already deal 6, and §2 states the thesis as "collision 6 vs attack 2". Unit collision at 4 was the outlier |
+| **Attack damage** | **unchanged at 2** | A Husk takes 3 swings or 1 shove; the mid-tier takes 5–6 swings or 2 shoves. **The board-over-sword gap goes from 2× to 3×** — the thesis stated louder, not compromised |
+| **Rest healing** | ~half → **25%** | A quarter cannot undo a bad fight, so damage accumulates and an early mistake is still felt at the boss |
+
+**Why collision had to move with enemy HP.** §6's teach is that *a bare collision still kills a
+Husk outright* — shove A into B and both die, the moment that sells pillar 1 to a new player. At
+Husk 6 with collision 4 that teach dies. At collision 6 it survives.
+
+**Re-derive, do not re-scale, everything downstream:** Preen still heals 4 (now **under 10%** of a
+Wardbearer, which *strengthens* its negative-sum design further — he erodes, he never fountains); Bedraggled's quarter-max (Archer **6**,
+Vanguard **11**); Guard Stance's halving unchanged; every mod and legendary naming a damage number.
+**Both bosses need re-tuning** — they now face ducks who survive their melee **three times** as often, which is the largest single consequence of this pass.
+
+**Rider, non-negotiable: §13's owed turn-limit audit (+1–2) lands in the SAME session.** Enemies at
+1.5× HP will start hitting limits written for the old pace, and a timing-out board is otherwise
+indistinguishable from a badly-tuned one. **Measure act length before and after; a large jump is a
+finding, not a success.**
